UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 28, Lesson 4: Presenting information', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou are practising presenting information. State the purpose, list two criteria and complete one guided example with a because-explanation.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise. Weak answers skip criteria or invent unsupported claims.\n\nCHECK\nThinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a substantial task on presenting information for a real classroom audience: plan, draft or perform as required, include evidence or sources where relevant, self-check against three criteria and write a five-sentence reflection naming one strength and one next goal.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and listening","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a substantial task on presenting information for a real classroom audience: plan, draft or perform as required, include evidence or sources where relevant, self-check against three criteria and wri Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using presenting information on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck.. Correction: Strong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating presenting information on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with presenting information, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about presenting information with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for presenting information. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for presenting information. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise"],"question":"You are practising presenting information. State the purpose, list two criteria and complete one guided example with a because-explanation. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for presenting information; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise","Careful use of evidence and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck","Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with presenting information?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named evidence and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck","Name evidence and explain why it matters only, without linking it to presenting information","Apply presenting information to new material and name the evidence and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies presenting information with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries presenting information. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show presenting information and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck"],"question":"Read: \"When the ferry horn blasted, Omar gripped the railing and laughed.\" Which choice best uses presenting information from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name evidence and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise","Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about presenting information?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse evidence and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for presenting information?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about presenting information"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of presenting information on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about presenting information with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating presenting information on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using presenting information on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for presenting information. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with presenting information, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck.. Correction: Strong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w28-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise presenting information.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use presenting information with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify evidence and explain why it matters.","I can practise purpose independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience check needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about presenting information with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short model with annotated steps, then checks a peer sample against the same criteria. Evidence is quoted or paraphrased accurately; fixes are specific.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou are practising presenting information. State the purpose, list two criteria and complete one guided example with a because-explanation.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise. Weak answers skip criteria or invent unsupported claims.","explain":"Year 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Presenting information.","caption":"See the process.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Teach</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what/why/how</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Model</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">worked example</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about presenting information with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short model with annotated steps, then checks a peer sample against the same criteria. Evidence is quoted or paraphrased accurately; fixes are specific. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Learning loop.","caption":"Evidence over guess.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Practise</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">guided then independent</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">criteria check</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about presenting information with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work. Here is a more productive model: Example: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short model with annotated steps, then checks a peer sample against the same criteria. Evidence is quoted or paraphrased accurately; fixes are specific.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Purpose clear; criteria fit the skill; explanation uses evidence, not vague praise. Weak answers skip criteria or invent unsupported claims.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Thinking presenting information is only talent or first-draft luck. Explicit process, evidence and revision produce reliable Year 7 work. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about presenting information with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nYear 7 students learn presenting information through explicit strategy, worked examples and evidence-based practice. Identify purpose and audience, apply a clear process, check work against criteria and explain choices with textual or factual support. Keep school-safe topics; credit sources; do not invent NESA content points beyond catalogue codes. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 28, Lesson 4: Presenting information","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Informative and explanatory texts","week":28,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: In a classroom task on presenting information, Sam names the purpose, lists two success criteria, works a short model with annotated steps, then checks a peer sample against the same criteria. Evidence is quoted or paraphrased accurately; fixes are specific.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Presenting information.","caption":"See the process.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Teach</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">what/why/how</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Model</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">worked example</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Learning loop.","caption":"Evidence over guess.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Practise</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">guided then independent</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">criteria check</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s4-eng-y7-w28-l4' AND family_id IS NULL;
